{{also|sénescent}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{uder|en|la|senescens}}, present participle of {{m|la|senescere||to grow [[old]]}}, from {{m|la|senere||to be old}}, from {{m|la|senex||old}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{rhymes|en|ɛsənt|s=3}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Growing [[old]]; decaying with the lapse of time.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Edgar Allan Poe|title=The Works of the Late Edgar Allan Poe|volume=2|year=1859|chapter=[[s:en:The Works of the Late Edgar Allan Poe/Volume 2/Ulalume|Ulalume]]|passage=And now, as the night was '''senescent''' / ⁠And star-dials pointed to morn— / ⁠As the star-dials hinted of morn— / At the end of our path a liquescent / ⁠And nebulous lustre was born}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Joseph Jastrow|work=Popular Science Monthly|volume=66|month=March|year=1905|title=[[s:en:Popular Science Monthly/Volume 66/March 1905/The Natural History of Adolescence|The Natural History of Adolescence]]|passage=The history of philosophic opinion itself in interpreted by Dr. Hall in terms of a similar development, in which immature adolescent systems, staid '''senescent''' and ''blasé'' philosophies have appeared and appealed to their public in direct relation to the status of the culture-periods in which they found origin and favor.}}
# [[characteristic|Characteristic]] of old [[age]].
# {{lb|en|cytology|of a cell}} That ceases to [[divide]].
#* {{quote-book|en|title=[[s:The Source and Aim of Human Progress|The Source and Aim of Human Progress]]|year=1919|author=Boris Sidis|passage=When such lines and forms of organic development prevail, the individual, as the cell of the body, becomes soon '''senescent''', drifting inevitably into age, decay, and death.}}

